[s0l0!-header][h1]The switch Statement[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

The [b]switch [/b]statement branches program control by matching the result of an expression with a constant [b]case[/b] value. 

The [b]switch [/b]statement often provides a more elegant solution to [b]if-else if[/b] and [b]nested if [/b]statements.
The switch takes the form: [code format="c" ][b]switch [/b](expression) {
  [b]case [/b]val1:
    statements
  break;
  [b]case [/b]val2:
    statements
  break;
  [b]default[/b]:
    statements
}[/code]
For example, the following program outputs "Three": [code format="c" codeId="1643"]int num = 3;

[b]  switch [/b](num) {
  [b]case [/b]1:
    printf("One\n");
    break;   
  [b]case [/b]2:
    printf("Two\n");
    break;   
  [b]case [/b]3:
    printf("Three\n");
    break;   
  [b]default[/b]:
    printf("Not 1, 2, or 3.\n");
}[/code]
[note]Run the code and see how it works![/note]

