[s0l0!-header][h1]The switch Statement[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

There can be multiple [b]cases [/b]with unique labels.
The optional [b]default [/b]case is executed when no other matches are made.
A [b]break [/b]statement is needed in each case to branch to the end of the [b]switch [/b]statement.
Without the [b]break [/b]statement, program execution falls through to the next [b]case [/b]statement. This can be useful when the same statement is needed for several cases. Consider the following [b]switch [/b]statement: [code format="c" codeId="1644"]switch (num) {
  case 1:
  case 2:
  case 3:
    printf("One, Two, or Three.\n");
    break;   
  case 4:
  case 5:
  case 6:
    printf("Four, Five, or Six.\n");
    break;
  default:
    printf("Greater than Six.\n");
}[/code]
Caution must be used when constructing the [b]switch [/b]this way. Modifications later can lead to unexpected outcomes.
[note]Run the code and see how it works![/note]

